the new norm. Sure, there are legal lines you can’t cross, but that’s no excuse to treat freelancers like the B-team. Loop them into meetings, hold regular one-on-ones, keep them in the same comms channels. The goal? No “us vs. them,” just one team, same mission.</span>\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12261" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Step-5-1024x317.png" alt="Step 5 is about Understanding what motivates them. Icon of a flag atop a bar chart. Respect flexibility and autonomy, set expectations, and avoid scope creep to unlock their best work." width="1024" height="317" />\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Yes, the paycheck matters. But so does flexibility, autonomy, and meaningful work. Respect the boundaries in your contractor agreements, avoid scope creep, and skip the “one more tiny change” requests that aren’t in the brief. Freelancers thrive when their work is valued - give them room to ramp up, set clear expectations, and they’ll bring their best.</span>\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12262" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Step-6-1024x318.png" alt="Step 6 is about enabling freelance talent: Provide tools, context, and feedback. Icon of a tool bag. Equip freelancers with full access, training, goals, and open communication to help them succeed." width="1024" height="318" />\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Tools, training, and context aren’t “nice-to-haves” - they’re essentials.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Set them up with full access and the “why” behind your business. Think:</span>\r\n\r\n<strong>Transparent comms</strong>\r\n\r\n<strong>A go-to person for questions</strong>\r\n\r\n<strong>Inclusive team culture</strong>\r\n\r\n<strong>Clarity on how their work ladders up to the mission</strong>\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12263" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-