(12294, 5, '2025-09-23 11:53:09', '2025-09-23 15:53:09', '<b>1) The Campaign Crunch is Real</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">If you’re leading a marketing or creative team, you know the feeling all too well: campaigns keep piling up, budgets keep shrinking, and somehow “do more with less” is still the rallying cry of the year. Full-time hiring, meanwhile, can feel like slogging through quicksand - slow, expensive, and risky. Filling a mid-level design role takes 30–45 days on average, while senior roles can stretch past 11 weeks. Add in recruiting, onboarding, and lost productivity, you’re out an extra $5K–$15K on top of salary. And if it’s the wrong fit? The ripple effects can drag on for months.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Having worked alongside marketing and creative leaders, I’ve seen this pattern repeat again and again. A lot of in-house teams grow the traditional way with full-time hires, but the reality is they often need to scale faster than headcount allows. And when the pressure’s on, traditional hiring models only slow momentum.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">That’s where Elastic Teams come in. They’re built for the realities of modern creative work - flexible, scalable, and designed to help teams deliver impact without adding unnecessary overhead. More than a temporary fix, they offer a way to build high-performing teams that can adjust as quickly as your business and market conditions demand.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>2) Why a Flexible Workforce Beats a Bloated One</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">The beauty of Elastic Teams? </span><span style="font-weight: 400;">You can scale up or down in a fraction of the time it takes to hire full-time</span><span style="font-weight: 400;"> - and even test roles before you commit to bringing them in-house. Pay for talent when you need it, skip the overhead when you don’t, and plug into specialists who can deliver on-demand (minus the high overhead costs).</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">But let’s be real: it’s not all rainbows and unicorns. Flexible talent comes with challenges - like workflow integration, communication gaps, extra management overhead, and sometimes compliance complexity. The companies that get it right don’t treat flex talent like outsiders. They invest in onboarding, build clear communication frameworks, and slot Elastic Teams into workflows as temporary extensions of the core team.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">That’s where a solid partner (<a href="https://worksandy.com/services/">hi!</a>) can step in - working alongside you to navigate challenges and provide the extra support that keeps your creative and marketing teams running smoothly.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>3) Bench Building: Your Secret Weapon</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Campaigns move at lightning speed - so why wait until you’re drowning to call for backup? Bench building is like future-proofing your sanity. As a creative director or marketing lead, you should always be curating portfolios of portfolios, so you’ve got the right talent mix ready when things heat up.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Think about it in three levels:</span>\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone wp-image-12289 size-large" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Section-3-Pyramid-4-V2-2-1024x493.png" alt="Triangle pyramid graphic showing tiers of creative support: top level “Strategic Talent (Creative Directors, Brand Writers)” focused on fresh thinking and shaping campaigns; middle level “Idea + Execution Crews (Photo teams, Video Production, Campaign Specialists)” translating vision into deliverables; base level “Executional Support (Designers, Production, Project Management)” handling overflow and production capacity." width="1024" height="493" />\r\n\r\n<b>Base: Executional support </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">(designers, production talent, project managers) who can quickly absorb overflow.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>Mid: Idea + execution crews</b><span style="font-weight: 400;"> (photo teams, video production, campaign specialists) who translate your vision into high-quality deliverables.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>High: Strategic talent </b><span style="font-weight: 400;">(creative directors, brand writers) who inject fresh thinking and elevate big-picture campaigns.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Mix and match across these tiers, and you’re never scrambling at the last minute for that copywriter, motion designer, or paid media wizard. Instead, you’re simply putting the pieces together with a stacked, vetted bench that’s ready to go.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>4) In-House Team = The Brain, Elastic Team = The Muscle</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Here’s the deal: Elastic Teams don’t replace your in-house crew - they can help amplify them. Think of your in-house team as the brain: they carry the institutional knowledge, know the quirks of your systems, and hold the brand DNA. Elastic Teams are the muscle: they add capacity, inject fresh thinking, and give you the ability to move faster when demand spikes.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Different organizations balance the two in different ways. </span><span style="font-weight: 400;">Some bring in senior flex talent pods, like strategists, creative directors, or producers, to dream up new ideas, while the in-house team handles execution</span><span style="font-weight: 400;"> because they’re closest to the tools, processes, and brand guardrails. </span><span style="font-weight: 400;">Others flip it: internal leadership sets the vision and the Elastic Team executes at speed, freeing your people to focus on strategy and stakeholder alignment.</span>\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone wp-image-12288 size-large" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Section-4-Two-Approaches-Table-V2-2-1024x516.png" alt="Infographic comparing Elastic-Led Vision and In-House-Led Vision models. Shows who leads strategy and who executes: Elastic-Led Vision uses flex talent pods for strategy while the in-house team executes; In-House-Led Vision has internal leadership set the vision while the Elastic Team executes." width="1024" height="516" />\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">There’s no one-size-fits-all approach. The key is staying flexible and honest about what your team really needs. Rule of thumb: your people lead, Elastic Teams can help deliver speed. But Elastic Teams can also be your creative spark - slotting in an AD/writer duo, for example, to pitch new ideas when your core team is tapped out.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">One important consideration? Internal dynamics. Your in-house team will naturally want to keep the “sexy” projects that feel most exciting and visible. Dropping in a hotshot flex team without clear communication can unintentionally frustrate your core crew. The best leaders manage expectations carefully: they frame Elastic Teams as a support system, and make it clear where each group shines. Sometimes, yes, you’ll call in the A-list to inject new thinking, but always with transparency so your internal folks feel valued and respected.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Handled well, Elastic Teams become a critical extension of your team, not a threat. They give your people breathing room, keep projects moving, and add innovation without stepping on toes.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>5) Plug &amp; Play: Workflow + Tools Integration</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">If your internal processes are solid, Elastic Teams can slide right into the systems you already use like Slack, Asana, Jira, or whatever keeps your projects moving. But it’s not as simple as handing over a login and hoping for the best. To really make this work, you’ll need to invest time and intention up front.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Here are a few steps to set your flex talent up for success:</span>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><span style="font-weight: 400;">Onboard like they’re your own. Share brand guidelines, workflows, and context so they’re not guessing.</span></li>\r\n 	<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><span style="font-weight: 400;">Map the flow of work. Clarify how assets will be shared, how feedback loops work, and who owns final approval.</span></li>\r\n 	<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><span style="font-weight: 400;">Build visibility in. Use shared dashboards, regular check-ins, and transparent reporting to keep everyone aligned.</span></li>\r\n 	<li style="font-weight: 400;" aria-level="1"><span style="font-weight: 400;">Assign a point person inside your in-house team who connects the dots and ensures the flex team isn’t operating in a silo.</span></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">When done right, your in-house team won’t feel like they’re babysitting, and your flex talent will perform like a true extension of your team. As we mentioned earlier, the organizations that succeed are the ones that hold temporary teammates to the same standards as full-timers - providing clarity, communication, and accountability each step of the way.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>6) Try Before You Buy: The Full-Time Conversion Perk</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">One perk that doesn’t get enough attention? </span><span style="font-weight: 400;">Elastic Teams can double as a built-in talent pipeline. When you find a freelancer or strategist who just gets it - culturally and creatively - you don’t need a six-month interview process. You’ve already seen them in action.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">It’s a bit like test-driving a car before you sign the lease - only this car can brainstorm, launch campaigns, and generate ROI in real time.</span>\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12290" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Section-6-Venn-Diagram-V2-1024x515.png" alt="Venn diagram comparing Elastic Teams/Freelancers and Full-Time Teams. The overlap highlights “Proven performers → talent pipeline,” showing how freelancers who succeed can transition into trusted, long-term team members." width="1024" height="515" />\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Of course, most partners will include a conversion fee if you decide to bring flex talent in-house. That fee usually decreases the longer you’ve worked with the person, and while you may pay a slight premium over FT costs upfront, you avoid the headaches and overhead of a full-time employee until you’re confident they’re the right fit.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">At Sandy, we see this as one of the most powerful parts of the model: you get access to amazing talent to fill immediate needs, with the option to seamlessly convert unicorns into permanent team members. It’s the perfect way to try before you buy with much less risk and way more upside.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>7) Wrap-Up: Future-Proofing Your Team</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Elastic Teams aren’t a quick fix - they’re a smarter, more resilient way to scale your creative operations. They help you bypass hiring delays, manage costs, and keep campaigns moving in a world that never slows down. Whether you’re an established in-house team that needs support during peak demands or a newer team building out key skills, the model adapts to where you are.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">At the end of the day, it’s not about adding layers of headcount - it’s about scaling with flexibility, speed, and the right talent at the right time.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Ready to see how Sandy can flex your team without breaking your budget? Let’s <a href="https://worksandy.com/find-talent/">talk</a>.</span>', 'Scaling Your Creative Team Without Full-Time Hires: The Power of Elastic Teams', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '12283-revision-v1', '', '', '2025-09-23 11:53:09', '2025-09-23 15:53:09', '', 12283, 'https://worksandy.com/?p=12294', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
(12295, 5, '2025-10-29 14:15:13', '2025-10-29 18:15:13', '<span style="font-weight: 400;">We recently had the chance to connect with some of the brightest minds in tech, media, and VC to explore how creativity and AI are reshaping the future of marketing. From conversations with leaders at Google, McKinsey, and Amazon to late-night discussions about where human imagination meets machine intelligence, one thing became clear: marketing is entering a new chapter.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Over the last two decades, marketing has evolved through major waves of change: print to digital, digital to social, social to creator. Now we’re stepping into the Human + AI era. It feels new, a little intimidating, and sometimes hard to make sense of, but the mission hasn’t changed. Our job is still to tell stories that connect people, build trust, and move culture forward. What’s different now is that imagination has more reach than ever. It shapes not just what we create, but how we create, and that’s changing everything about how brands grow and connect.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">You could feel it in the air at LA Tech Week. What stood out wasn’t just the technology but the energy in every room. It felt like the early dot-com days with that same spark of possibility, but this time with a stronger sense of shared purpose. There’s excitement, yes, but also awareness. Everyone knows this next wave of innovation comes with responsibility. Ethical use, creative integrity, and human impact were a big part of the conversation.</span>\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12303" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Collage-w_-Shadows-1024x669.png" alt="Photo collage from Google’s “Culture Meets AI” sessions during LA Tech Week, featuring the Sandy team with event speakers and attendees. Images show panels on AI and creativity, industry leaders discussing the state of the LA AI ecosystem, and candid moments of collaboration and connection." width="1024" height="669" />\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">It’s easy to assume AI might one day replace human creators, but the shared belief across every conversation was the opposite. AI isn’t here to take over creativity, but to collaborate with it. It gives marketers a bigger canvas, faster tools, and new ways to understand and connect with audiences. But the real creativity still comes from us: the strategy, ideas, and storytelling that turn smart tools into emotional, meaningful work that moves people.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Here are five creative and marketing themes we took away from those conversations that every marketing leader should be thinking about as we build toward the Human + AI era.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>1) Human + AI Storytelling: Creativity as Collaboration</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">The most powerful stories still start with people - their ideas, emotions, and lived experiences. What’s changing is how quickly those stories can come to life. AI tools are helping creative teams move from concept to creation in hours instead of weeks, giving more time to refine the story rather than fight the process.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">As one creative director shared, </span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">“We used to spend 80% of our time executing ideas. Now, with AI, we can spend 80% of our time improving them.”</span></i>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">AI is changing how we tell stories, not why we tell them. One panelist put it perfectly: </span>\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12298" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Quote-1-Color-1024x512.png" alt="Graphic quote reading “AI can generate a scene, but only humans can give it a soul,” displayed in bold navy blue text with large light-grey quotation marks in the background." width="1024" height="512" />\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Producer Jess Engel called it “art directing a machine,” describing AI as a tool that still demands taste, intuition, and emotional resonance. </span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">For marketers, the message is clear: AI can expand what’s possible, but it’s still up to us to direct the story.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>2) AI for Brand Storytelling: Expanding the Canvas</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">AI isn’t just changing how we create, it’s changing how brands tell their stories. The creative canvas is growing, giving marketers new ways to understand audiences, test ideas, and bring stories to life faster than ever before.</span>\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12323" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Quote-2-Color-1-1024x512.png" alt="Quote graphic reading “AI isn’t replacing creators; it’s giving them more time to tell better stories.” — Julia Yan, Former Head of Growth at TikTok. The text appears in bold navy blue on a white background with large light-teal quotation marks behind it." width="1024" height="512" />\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">We’re already seeing this in action. From dynamic creative testing and personalized video production to voice synthesis and adaptive design, AI is helping brands express themselves in new and unexpected ways. The challenge isn’t to automate creativity -  it’s to humanize it at scale.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Today, even small teams can create cinematic, emotionally resonant campaigns that once required massive budgets. Visual generation and video-to-video transformation tools are leveling the playing field, empowering marketers to produce story-driven content that feels as polished as a studio production.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">At its best, AI helps audiences feel more seen, not more targeted.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>3) AI in Action: How AI Bringing Ideas to Life</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Across industries, AI is already reshaping how ideas move from concept to campaign.</span>\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12319" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Quote-3-Color-1-1024x512.png" alt="Quote graphic featuring the text: “We can now test creative across synthetic audiences of thousands in days rather than six-month focus groups.” — Ryan Hammill, Creative Director at ServiceNow. The quote appears in bold navy text with large beige quotation marks in the background." width="1024" height="512" />\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">That kind of speed and scale gives brands unprecedented feedback loops and creative clarity.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">In e-commerce, Rita from <a href="https://www.unique-vintage.com/">Unique Vintage</a> explained, “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Meta wants 15 versions of every ad. We have one designer. Without AI, we can’t scale.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">” Her team now uses AI to generate variations while maintaining brand voice and emotional impact.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">In film, where ambitious ideas once required massive budgets and studio teams, AI is shifting the focus from resources to vision. The creative director of <a href="https://www.primordialsoup.ai/">Primordial Soup</a> produced a striking short film about the Voyager Golden Record in just three weeks using only AI tools - proof that storytelling craft now matters more than access to gear.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Agencies are evolving too. “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">We used to shoot on location. Now we just shoot clean white studio and AI does the rest</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">,” said Neil from KOE Agency. The result: faster campaigns, smaller budgets, and more creative freedom.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">This power to create high-concept work with fewer resources is redefining how both filmmakers and brands bring ideas to life. Whether it’s directors rendering entire scenes in days or marketers testing dozens of concepts in real time, one thing is clear - AI isn’t replacing creativity; it’s accelerating it.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>4) Human Skills: More Valuable Than Ever</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">As impressive as AI has become, a consistent theme across all the LA Tech Week panels was that human skills have never been more valuable. AI can generate, optimize, and accelerate, but it can’t feel tension, read a room, or know when to break the rules. That’s where we come in.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Taste, intuition, emotional intelligence, and strategic clarity will define the creative leaders of this new era. The best teams won’t be guided by AI, they’ll drive it, using these tools as extensions of their imagination.</span>\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12320" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Table-1024x1024.png" alt="A two-column chart comparing AI’s role and human creativity in marketing. The left column, titled “AI’s Role (The Tool),” lists functions like generating visual concepts, testing message variations, automating asset creation, and accelerating production. The right column, titled “Our Role (The Storyteller),” highlights the human side: providing vision and taste, defining emotional truth, deciding why assets matter, and ensuring cultural meaning and emotional depth." width="1024" height="1024" />\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Ryan Hammill, Creative Director at <a href="https://www.servicenow.com/">ServiceNow</a>, captured it well: “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">We’re reaching parity for craft. Taste is the new differentiator.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">”</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">As AI makes technical execution more accessible, the value shifts toward what only humans can provide: curiosity, empathy, originality, and the ability to give ideas meaning. Success won’t come from mastering every new tool but from cultivating the judgment to know which ideas are worth bringing to life.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Ultimately, the future of creativity isn’t human versus AI. It’s about partnership, a blend of human vision and machine intelligence that allows stories to connect more deeply than ever before.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>5) Trust: The Ultimate Differentiator</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">In a world flooded with AI-driven content, authenticity and trust are what set brands apart. People want to believe in the stories they’re told. They want to know there is intent, honesty, and consideration behind what they see.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">That’s why transparency matters. How data is used, how content is created, and how brands express their values through technology all shape the trust they build with their audiences. The more automated marketing becomes, the more people will crave what feels genuine and human.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Adriana Gosford, CMO of <a href="https://artera.io/">Artera</a>, put it best:</span>\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12301" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Quote-4-Color-1024x512.png" alt="Quote graphic reading “AI is a club that lets too many people in with fake IDs.” — Adriana Gosford, CMO of Artera. The text appears in bold navy lettering over a white background with large, light-grey quotation marks behind it." width="1024" height="512" />\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">It’s a reminder that not everything labeled </span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">“AI-powered</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">” deserves our confidence. As creative leaders, we have a responsibility to use these tools with clarity, honesty, and intention.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">That sentiment echoed across the Google AI sessions. Responsible design, fairness in data, and inclusion in model training aren’t just ethical imperatives - they’re business imperatives. Othmane Rifki of <a href="https://www.meta.ai/">Meta AI</a> shared, “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Without diverse voices in the room, it’s impossible to catch where the models go wrong.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">”</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Trust is not built by claiming responsibility. It is earned by demonstrating it, through every decision, every story, and every use of technology that puts people first.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>Bringing It All Together</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">It’s hard to wrap up a week’s worth of conversations into one post, but the takeaway for us was simple. AI is transforming how creative work gets done, and it’s happening faster than ever. But the real opportunity lies in how we blend human creativity with machine intelligence.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">The most exciting part isn’t the technology itself, but what creative people will do with it. We’ll be able to understand audiences better, create content faster, and tell more compelling stories for less, but that only matters if we have the right storytellers - people who know how to use these tools to sharpen their thinking, strengthen their ideas, and make their work more meaningful.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">We found ourselves quoting Ryan at <a href="https://www.servicenow.com/">ServiceNow</a>, quite a bit - and for good reason, but one of his most powerful takeaways was this</span>\r\n\r\n<img class="alignnone size-large wp-image-12321" src="https://worksandy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Quote-5-Color-1-1024x512.png" alt="Quote graphic reading “The differentiator soon won’t be the tools, it’ll be your people.” — Ryan Hammill, Creative Director at ServiceNow. The text is in bold navy blue on a white background with large soft pink quotation marks behind it." width="1024" height="512" />\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">And Othmane Rifki, Research Scientist at <a href="https://www.meta.ai/">Meta AI</a>, echoed that same sentiment, “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">AI is not replacing human intelligence, it’s augmenting it. But people who use AI will replace people who don’t.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">”</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Technology levels the playing field, but what sets brands apart is their people - the thinkers, makers, and storytellers who know how to combine creativity with strategy and use AI as a catalyst for better ideas. The differentiator isn’t the tools; it’s the talent behind them.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">At <a href="https://worksandy.com/services/">Sandy</a>, we help marketing and creative leaders build AI-ready teams that combine strategy, creativity, and technology in powerful new ways. Because the future of marketing isn’t human or AI, it’s both - working together to tell stories that move the world forward.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Ready to see how Sandy can help you level up with AI-ready creative talent? <a href="https://worksandy.com/find-talent/">Let’s talk</a>.</span>', 'LA Tech Week Recap: 5 Creative Themes Shaping the Future of AI and Marketing', '', 'publish', 'open', 'open', '', 'la-tech-week-recap-5-creative-themes-shaping-the-future-of-ai-and-marketing', '', '', '2025-10-30 18:09:54', '2025-10-30 22:09:54', '', 0, 'https://worksandy.com/?p=12295', 0, 'post', '', 0) ;
INSERT INTO `ULhz1h_posts` ( `ID`, `post_author`, `post_date`, `post_date_gmt`, `post_content`, `post_title`, `post_excerpt`, `post_status`, `comment_status`, `ping_status`, `post_password`, `post_name`, `to_ping`, `pinged`, `post_modified`, `post_modified_gmt`, `post_content_filtered`, `post_parent`, `guid`, `menu_order`, `post_type`, `post_mime_type`, `comment_count`) VALUES
(12296, 5, '2025-10-29 13:14:11', '2025-10-29 17:14:11', '<span style="font-weight: 400;">We recently had the chance to connect with some of the brightest minds in tech, media, and VC to explore how creativity and AI are reshaping the future of marketing. From conversations with leaders at Google, McKinsey, and Amazon to late-night discussions about where human imagination meets machine intelligence, one thing became clear: marketing is entering a new chapter.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Over the last two decades, marketing has evolved through major waves of change: print to digital, digital to social, social to creator. Now we’re stepping into the Human + AI era. It feels new, a little intimidating, and sometimes hard to make sense of, but the mission hasn’t changed. Our job is still to tell stories that connect people, build trust, and move culture forward. What’s different now is that imagination has more reach than ever. It shapes not just what we create, but how we create, and that’s changing everything about how brands grow and connect.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">You could feel it in the air at LA Tech Week. What stood out wasn’t just the technology but the energy in every room. It felt like the early dot-com days with that same spark of possibility, but this time with a stronger sense of shared purpose. There’s excitement, yes, but also awareness. Everyone knows this next wave of innovation comes with responsibility. Ethical use, creative integrity, and human impact were a big part of the conversation.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">It’s easy to assume AI might one day replace human creators, but the shared belief across every conversation was the opposite. AI isn’t here to take over creativity, but to collaborate with it. It gives marketers a bigger canvas, faster tools, and new ways to understand and connect with audiences. But the real creativity still comes from us: the strategy, ideas, and storytelling that turn smart tools into emotional, meaningful work that moves people.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Here are five creative and marketing themes we took away from those conversations that every marketing leader should be thinking about as we build toward the Human + AI era.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>1) Human + AI Storytelling: Creativity as Collaboration</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">The most powerful stories still start with people - their ideas, emotions, and lived experiences. What’s changing is how quickly those stories can come to life. AI tools are helping creative teams move from concept to creation in hours instead of weeks, giving more time to refine the story rather than fight the process.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">As one creative director shared, </span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">“We used to spend 80% of our time executing ideas. Now, with AI, we can spend 80% of our time improving them.”</span></i>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">AI is changing how we tell stories, not why we tell them. One panelist put it perfectly: “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">AI can generate a scene, but only humans can give it a soul.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">”</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Producer Jess Engel of Primordial Soup called it “art directing a machine,” describing AI as a tool that still demands taste, intuition, and emotional resonance. </span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">For marketers, the message is clear: AI can expand what’s possible, but it’s still up to us to direct the story.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>2) AI for Brand Storytelling: Expanding the Canvas</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">AI isn’t just changing how we create, it’s changing how brands tell their stories. The creative canvas is growing, giving marketers new ways to understand audiences, test ideas, and bring stories to life faster than ever before.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Julia Lan, former Head of Growth at TikTok, shared, “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">AI isn’t replacing creators; it’s giving them more time to tell better stories</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">.”</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">We’re already seeing this in action. From dynamic creative testing and personalized video production to voice synthesis and adaptive design, AI is helping brands express themselves in new and unexpected ways. The challenge isn’t to automate creativity -  it’s to humanize it at scale.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Today, even small teams can create cinematic, emotionally resonant campaigns that once required massive budgets. Visual generation and video-to-video transformation tools are leveling the playing field, empowering marketers to produce story-driven content that feels as polished as a studio production.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">At its best, AI helps audiences feel more seen, not more targeted.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>3) AI in Action: How AI Bringing Ideas to Life</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Across industries, AI is already reshaping how ideas move from concept to campaign.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Ryan Hamill, Creative Director at ServiceNow, shared, “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">We can now test creative across synthetic audiences of thousands in days rather than six-month focus groups.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">” That kind of speed and scale gives brands unprecedented feedback loops and creative clarity.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">In e-commerce, Rina from Unique Vintage explained, “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Meta wants 15 versions of every ad. We have one designer. Without AI, we can’t scale.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">” Her team now uses AI to generate variations while maintaining brand voice and emotional impact.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">In film, where ambitious ideas once required massive budgets and studio teams, AI is shifting the focus from resources to vision. The creative director of Primordial Soup produced a striking short film about the Voyager Golden Record in just three weeks using only AI tools - proof that storytelling craft now matters more than access to gear.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Agencies are evolving too. “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">We used to shoot on location. Now we just shoot clean white studio and AI does the rest</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">,” said Neil from KOE Agency. The result: faster campaigns, smaller budgets, and more creative freedom.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">This power to create high-concept work with fewer resources is redefining how both filmmakers and brands bring ideas to life. Whether it’s directors rendering entire scenes in days or marketers testing dozens of concepts in real time, one thing is clear - AI isn’t replacing creativity; it’s accelerating it.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>4) Human Skills: More Valuable Than Ever</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">As impressive as AI has become, a consistent theme across all the LA Tech Week panels was that human skills have never been more valuable. AI can generate, optimize, and accelerate, but it can’t feel tension, read a room, or know when to break the rules. That’s where we come in.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Taste, intuition, emotional intelligence, and strategic clarity will define the creative leaders of this new era. The best teams won’t be guided by AI, they’ll drive it, using these tools as extensions of their imagination.</span>\r\n<table>\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td><b>The AI\'s Role (The Tool)</b></td>\r\n<td><b>The Human\'s Role (The Storyteller)</b></td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td><span style="font-weight: 400;">Generating thousands of visual concepts</span></td>\r\n<td><span style="font-weight: 400;">Providing the vision and taste to choose what works</span></td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td><span style="font-weight: 400;">Testing message variations on synthetic audiences</span></td>\r\n<td><span style="font-weight: 400;">Defining the core emotional truth the message must convey</span></td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td><span style="font-weight: 400;">Automating the creation of a digital asset</span></td>\r\n<td><span style="font-weight: 400;">Deciding why those assets are needed and how they’ll connect</span></td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td><span style="font-weight: 400;">Accelerating the production pipeline</span></td>\r\n<td><span style="font-weight: 400;">Ensuring the final story has cultural meaning and emotional depth</span></td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Ryan Hamill, Creative Director at ServiceNow, captured it best: “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">We’re reaching parity for craft. Taste is the new differentiator.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">”</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">As AI makes technical execution more accessible, the value shifts toward what only humans can provide: curiosity, empathy, originality, and the ability to give ideas meaning. Success won’t come from mastering every new tool but from cultivating the judgment to know which ideas are worth bringing to life.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Ultimately, the future of creativity isn’t human versus AI. It’s about partnership, a blend of human vision and machine intelligence that allows stories to connect more deeply than ever before.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>5) Trust: The Ultimate Differentiator</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">In a world flooded with AI-driven content, authenticity and trust are what set brands apart. People want to believe in the stories they’re told. They want to know there is intent, honesty, and consideration behind what they see.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">That’s why transparency matters. How data is used, how content is created, and how brands express their values through technology all shape the trust they build with their audiences. The more automated marketing becomes, the more people will crave what feels genuine and human.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Adriana Gosford, CMO of Artera, put it best: “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">AI is a club that lets too many people in with fake IDs.” </span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">It’s a reminder that not everything labeled </span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">“AI-powered</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">” deserves our confidence. As creative leaders, we have a responsibility to use these tools with clarity, honesty, and intention.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">That sentiment echoed across the Google AI sessions. Responsible design, fairness in data, and inclusion in model training aren’t just ethical imperatives - they’re business imperatives. Othmane Rifki of Meta AI shared, “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">Without diverse voices in the room, it’s impossible to catch where the models go wrong.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">”</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Trust is not built by claiming responsibility. It is earned by demonstrating it, through every decision, every story, and every use of technology that puts people first.</span>\r\n\r\n<b>Bringing It All Together</b>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">It’s hard to wrap up a week’s worth of conversations into one post, but the takeaway for us was simple. AI is transforming how creative work gets done, and it’s happening faster than ever. But the real opportunity lies in how we blend human creativity with machine intelligence.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">The most exciting part isn’t the technology itself, but what creative people will do with it. We’ll be able to understand audiences better, create content faster, and tell more compelling stories for less, but that only matters if we have the right storytellers - people who know how to use these tools to sharpen their thinking, strengthen their ideas, and make their work more meaningful.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">We found ourselves quoting Ryan Hamill, Creative Director at ServiceNow, quite a bit - and for good reason, but one of his most powerful takeaways was, “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">The differentiator soon won’t be the tools, it’ll be your people.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">”</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">And Othmane Rifki, Research Scientist at Meta AI, echoed that same sentiment, “</span><i><span style="font-weight: 400;">AI is not replacing human intelligence, it’s augmenting it. But people who use AI will replace people who don’t.</span></i><span style="font-weight: 400;">”</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Technology levels the playing field, but what sets brands apart is their people - the thinkers, makers, and storytellers who know how to combine creativity with strategy and use AI as a catalyst for better ideas. The differentiator isn’t the tools; it’s the talent behind them.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">At Sandy, we help marketing and creative leaders build AI-ready teams that combine strategy, creativity, and technology in powerful new ways. Because the future of marketing isn’t human or AI, it’s both - working together to tell stories that move the world forward.</span>\r\n\r\n<span style="font-weight: 400;">Ready to see how Sandy can help you level up with AI-ready creative talent? Let’s talk.</span>', 'LA Tech Week Recap: 5 Creative Themes Shaping the Future of AI + Marketing', '', 'inherit', 'closed', 'closed', '', '12295-revision-v1', '', '', '2025-10-29 13:14:11', '2025-10-29 17:14:11', '', 12295, 'https://worksandy.com/?p=12296', 0, 'revision', '', 0),
